course: dictionary
lesson: 13817
title: Irreversible: cannot be changed back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Irreversible is an adjective that means not able to be changed back to how it was before. The start of the word, ir-, means not, and reversible means able to be turned back, so irreversible means it cannot be turned back.
explain: In science, you can tell whether a change is irreversible by asking, «Can I get the material back the way it was?» Burning paper makes ash, and you can never get the paper back, so it is an irreversible change. Melting ice is different: freeze the water again and you get ice back, so it is reversible.
explain: The word is also used about harm and damage. «The rust caused irreversible damage to the old bridge.» If the damage cannot be repaired or undone, the reader knows the bridge will never be the way it was. Look for clues like damage, change or harm to help you spot the word.
fact: Irreversible means not able to be changed back to how it was before.
fact: The opposite is reversible, which means it can be changed back.
fact: Burning paper, cooking an egg and rusting iron are irreversible changes.
fact: Melting ice is reversible, because freezing the water makes ice again.
fact: Irreversible damage is harm that cannot be undone, such as damage to the environment or to your health.
exercise: «Once the egg is cooked, it cannot become raw again, so the change is irreversible.» What does irreversible mean? | Easy to change back | *Cannot be changed back to how it was before | Very hot | Made of eggs
exercise: Which of these is a reversible change? | Burning a candle | *Melting ice into water | Rusting a nail | Cooking an egg
exercise: «The flood caused irreversible damage to the village.» Which word tells you the damage cannot be undone? | caused | *irreversible | flood | village
test: Which of these is an irreversible change? | Water freezing into ice | Ice melting into water | Pouring water into a cup | *Burning paper into ash
test: «The doctor said the harm to his hearing was irreversible.» What does this tell you? | *The hearing can never be fully restored | The doctor is cross with him | The hearing test was fun | The hearing will get better next week
exam: Maya says melting ice is irreversible, so it can never be changed back. Is she right? | Yes, once ice melts it is gone for ever | Not sure, because ice is cold | *No, freezing the water turns it back into ice, so the change is reversible | Yes, because water is wet
